# Commits

Do not commit unless the person asks. When they ask, use the fewest commits that keep each idea alone. Not one commit per file or tweak.

The message has a type, is written in English, and does not mention AI tools.

```
type(module): what changed and why
```

Module (product area): `onboarding`, `discovery`, `affinity`, `connect`, `learn`, `backend`. If the change spans several areas, omit the parentheses.

| Type | When |
| --- | --- |
| `feat` | New behavior |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `refactor` | Internal change, behavior stays the same |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `test` | Tests |
| `chore` | Maintenance that does not change the product |
| `perf` | Performance |
| `ci` | Continuous integration |

```
feat(discovery): keep the camera on the selected person so a second look stays put
fix(connect): open the public card when the QR is scanned
docs: commit message rules with feat and fix types
```

No `update`, bare `fix`, `wip`, or empty subjects. One logical change per commit. Do not commit `.env`, `.env.local`, keys, `.data/`, `node_modules/`, or `.next/`.

Human detail lives in `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
